1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,303
It's project time.

2
00:00:02,653 --> 00:00:06,706
You've learned four kinds of
memory, and how to store them safely.

3
00:00:07,056 --> 00:00:08,739
Now we put them together.

4
00:00:09,089 --> 00:00:14,711
Over two lessons, you'll build a study
companion that remembers you across days,

5
00:00:14,711 --> 00:00:20,263
keeps its conversations small, and
lets you see and delete everything it knows.

6
00:00:22,063 --> 00:00:23,476
Here's what we're building.

7
00:00:23,826 --> 00:00:29,756
A study companion you talk to at the
command line: one message with ask, or a whole

8
00:00:29,756 --> 00:00:30,471
chat loop.

9
00:00:31,071 --> 00:00:36,476
It saves lasting facts with tools:
your name, your goals, what you find hard.

10
00:00:37,076 --> 00:00:42,659
Conversations are saved and resume
where you left off, and old turns fold into a

11
00:00:42,659 --> 00:00:43,775
running summary.

12
00:00:44,375 --> 00:00:49,067
Everything lives in SQLite,
with each user's data kept apart.

13
00:00:49,667 --> 00:00:55,156
You can see everything it knows, forget
a fact, purge old chats, or delete it all.

14
00:00:55,506 --> 00:00:58,848
And there are forty-one
tests, with an offline mode.

15
00:01:00,648 --> 00:01:01,778
Here's the plan.

16
00:01:02,128 --> 00:01:07,861
The command line opens the companion for
one user and one session, and passes each

17
00:01:07,861 --> 00:01:09,329
message to the agent.

18
00:01:09,929 --> 00:01:14,623
The agent keeps the conversation:
recent turns, and the running summary.

19
00:01:14,973 --> 00:01:18,120
That's short-term memory,
from lessons two and three.

20
00:01:18,720 --> 00:01:22,776
The model reaches long-term
memory only through three tools.

21
00:01:23,126 --> 00:01:29,758
Memory dot py holds the facts, and
everything is stored by db dot py, in SQLite.

22
00:01:30,358 --> 00:01:35,981
And the client talks to the real Claude
API when there's a key, or to the scripted

23
00:01:35,981 --> 00:01:36,941
fake, offline.

24
00:01:38,741 --> 00:01:42,931
Settings come from
environment variables, as in every module.

25
00:01:43,281 --> 00:01:48,222
No API key means offline mode,
and keys are never written in code.

26
00:01:48,822 --> 00:01:52,004
COMP home is the
folder for the database file.

27
00:01:52,604 --> 00:01:56,116
Keep turns: how many
recent turns stay word for word.

28
00:01:56,466 --> 00:02:00,649
And summarize at: the
estimated size that triggers a summary.

29
00:02:00,998 --> 00:02:03,476
You can tune both without touching code.

30
00:02:04,076 --> 00:02:08,612
And COMP today fixes the date,
so tests and demos are repeatable.

31
00:02:10,412 --> 00:02:14,159
The offline fake is
built to teach memory honestly.

32
00:02:14,509 --> 00:02:19,903
Like a real model, it only knows what's
in the request: the system prompt, with the

33
00:02:19,903 --> 00:02:22,243
summary and facts, and the messages.

34
00:02:22,593 --> 00:02:25,241
Drop the history, and
it can't know your name.

35
00:02:25,841 --> 00:02:27,501
It follows a fixed script.

36
00:02:27,850 --> 00:02:33,644
Phrases like my name is, or I find
something hard, become remember fact calls.

37
00:02:33,994 --> 00:02:36,364
Forget becomes recall, then forget.

38
00:02:36,964 --> 00:02:42,386
And every reply is labelled offline
demo, and every summary offline summary.

39
00:02:42,736 --> 00:02:45,306
It's never presented as model output.

40
00:02:47,106 --> 00:02:49,171
The command line opens a session.

41
00:02:49,521 --> 00:02:55,223
By default, the session is today's
date, so each day starts a fresh conversation,

42
00:02:55,223 --> 00:02:57,546
while long-term facts carry over.

43
00:02:58,146 --> 00:03:01,835
Creating the companion loads
the session from the database.

44
00:03:02,185 --> 00:03:06,536
If it has turns already, it's
resumed, and the first line says so.

45
00:03:07,136 --> 00:03:12,531
And chat is a read eval print loop:
read a line, answer, until you type bye.

46
00:03:12,881 --> 00:03:16,381
Piped input works too,
which makes demos repeatable.

47
00:03:18,181 --> 00:03:19,119
Let's run it.

48
00:03:19,469 --> 00:03:24,278
Every command is a separate
process, like closing and reopening the app.

49
00:03:24,878 --> 00:03:30,620
First, a clean start: remove the data
folder, so the database is created fresh.

50
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:34,310
Now a first message,
in a session called mon.

51
00:03:34,660 --> 00:03:35,735
A new session.

52
00:03:36,085 --> 00:03:41,654
The model calls remember fact twice, in
one turn: a profile fact, and a struggle.

53
00:03:42,004 --> 00:03:46,211
Then a short reply, and the
footer shows the conversation size.

54
00:03:46,811 --> 00:03:49,058
A second message, in the same session.

55
00:03:49,408 --> 00:03:52,587
It's resumed with one
turn, and a goal is saved.

56
00:03:53,187 --> 00:03:58,672
The memory command shows everything
remembered about you: three facts, with topics

57
00:03:58,672 --> 00:03:59,340
and dates.

58
00:03:59,690 --> 00:04:01,255
Visible, as promised.

59
00:04:01,855 --> 00:04:04,811
And the chat loop,
continuing the same session.

60
00:04:05,161 --> 00:04:06,596
What did we talk about?

61
00:04:06,946 --> 00:04:12,261
It can see both earlier turns, because
the session was loaded from the database.

62
00:04:14,061 --> 00:04:15,218
Pause and try.

63
00:04:15,568 --> 00:04:19,731
Tomorrow, you run ask, what's
my name, without a session option.

64
00:04:20,331 --> 00:04:21,827
Which session is used?

65
00:04:22,177 --> 00:04:23,471
What's in the request?

66
00:04:23,821 --> 00:04:25,124
And can it answer?

67
00:04:31,724 --> 00:04:35,177
The session is tomorrow's
date, so it's new and empty.

68
00:04:35,527 --> 00:04:40,853
But the system prompt still carries the
long-term facts, including: the learner's

69
00:04:40,853 --> 00:04:41,707
name is Anuj.

70
00:04:42,057 --> 00:04:44,030
So yes, it answers Anuj.

71
00:04:44,380 --> 00:04:48,866
That's the difference between
conversation memory and long-term memory.

72
00:04:50,666 --> 00:04:51,807
Interview corner.

73
00:04:52,157 --> 00:04:55,797
What's the difference between
a session, and long-term memory?

74
00:04:58,397 --> 00:05:04,520
A good answer: a session is one
conversation, its turns and its summary, which you

75
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:06,089
can resume by its ID.

76
00:05:06,439 --> 00:05:10,908
Long-term memory is facts about
the user, shared by all sessions.

77
00:05:11,258 --> 00:05:15,242
A new session starts with an
empty history, but the same facts.

78
00:05:15,592 --> 00:05:19,990
The trap is keeping everything in
one conversation that grows forever.

79
00:05:20,590 --> 00:05:21,527
Follow-up.

80
00:05:21,877 --> 00:05:26,485
How do you test an agent's
memory without paying for API calls?

81
00:05:29,085 --> 00:05:35,253
Run the real agent, the real tools and
a real database against a fake API that

82
00:05:35,253 --> 00:05:40,473
speaks the same protocol, with a
temporary database for each test.

83
00:05:40,823 --> 00:05:45,433
Then check the facts saved, the
requests sent, and the sessions reloaded.

84
00:05:45,783 --> 00:05:51,015
The trap is mocking the agent's own
methods, so the real loop is never tested.

85
00:05:52,815 --> 00:05:53,673
Quick check.

86
00:05:54,023 --> 00:05:59,211
In the companion, which part decides
that, I find recursion hard, is worth

87
00:05:59,211 --> 00:06:00,052
remembering?

88
00:06:06,252 --> 00:06:08,605
The model, by calling remember fact.

89
00:06:08,955 --> 00:06:11,098
Your code then checks it, and stores it.

90
00:06:12,898 --> 00:06:13,987
Let's recap.

91
00:06:14,337 --> 00:06:20,194
The layers: the command line, the
agent, the conversation, and tools, memory and

92
00:06:20,194 --> 00:06:20,707
SQLite.

93
00:06:21,307 --> 00:06:25,677
Settings come from the
environment, and it runs offline without a key.

94
00:06:26,277 --> 00:06:29,988
The fake is honest: it only
knows what's in the request.

95
00:06:30,588 --> 00:06:35,601
Sessions resume from the database,
and facts are shared by all sessions.

96
00:06:36,201 --> 00:06:39,472
Next lesson: the
agent's turn, line by line.

97
00:06:39,822 --> 00:06:45,802
Then a two-day demo, privacy features on
purpose, the tests, and the module wrap-up.
